target/
**/*.rs.bk
*.swp

# fuzz corpora and artifacts: the targets are tracked, what they find is not
fuzz/target/
fuzz/corpus/
fuzz/artifacts/

# Local editor and tooling state. Nothing under these belongs in history.
.vscode/
.idea/

# The C client and the GUI build in place, the way the sibling projects do.
client/*.o
client/*.a
client/.build-flags
client/tests/client_test
gui/build/
gui/build-*/

# The C port builds in place too, one object beside each source and one test
# binary per module. Named by shape rather than by module: the tree grows a
# module at a time and a list here would be one more thing to remember.
# Two levels, because `c/Makefile`'s own SRCS is two levels: the backends are
# `src/backend/<daemon>/*.c`, one directory per daemon, and their objects land
# beside them. This said one level until twelve backend objects were committed
# as source -- `make -C c clean` then deleted tracked files, and every build
# left the tree dirty for whoever else was working in it.
c/src/*/*.o
c/src/*/*/*.o
# The shared JSON reader's object, which `c/` builds under its own name rather
# than beside the source in `client/` -- one file, two builds, two objects, so
# that a sanitized build here cannot poison the archive `client/` links.
c/ncfg_json.o
# What `c/` was last built with, so a flag change rebuilds rather than relinks
# instrumented objects against no runtime.
c/.build-flags
c/*.a
c/tests/*_test
# The program the port links, and its second name. Two literal names rather
# than a shape, because `c/` is a source directory and everything else in it is
# tracked -- a pattern here would be a promise about whatever lands next.
c/netcfgd
c/ncfg
gui/tests/build/
# The live GUI probe builds beside its own project file, which is in a
# subdirectory so that `make -C gui test` does not discover and run it without
# a daemon.
gui/tests/live/build/

# Built packages. The recipes and the metadata are tracked; the .deb and .apk
# they produce are not -- a package is a build artifact of a specific commit,
# and `make deb` reproduces it from that commit.
dist/

# debhelper's working state; debian/ holds packaging sources only.
debian/.debhelper/
debian/files
debian/tmp/
debian/netcfgd/
# The other binary packages' staging trees. Named rather than globbed as
# `debian/netcfgd*/`, which would also swallow debian/netcfgd.postinst,
# debian/netcfgd-modem.postrm and any other per-package packaging source
# somebody adds later -- the rule above exists because debhelper's working
# state was committed once already, and a glob here would hide the maintainer
# scripts that are the packaging.
debian/netcfgd-nm/
debian/netcfgd-gui/
debian/netcfgd-modem/
debian/netcfgd-trinity/

# Python's own, beside whatever ran. `tools/` holds three gates now and
# importing one leaves this; it is not build output anybody named, which is
# why it is ignored rather than cleaned.
__pycache__/
debian/*.substvars
# The snippets dh_installsystemd and friends generate into maintainer scripts,
# as well as the log. `*.debhelper.log` alone missed `*.debhelper`, so
# `netcfgd.postrm.debhelper` sat in `git status` after every `make deb` -- and
# a line that is always there and never explained is what trains somebody to
# skim the list they are supposed to read before committing.
debian/*.debhelper
debian/*.debhelper.log
debian/debhelper-build-stamp

# fmake's object cache and the client it leaves at the top level.
# make writes its own build output under build/ instead.
.fmake/
/netcfgd-gui

# The TDE adapter builds with CMake, which wants its own binary directory.
adapter/netcfgd-tde/build/
